{{-- Aurora guidelines for AI code assistants --}}

## Aurora CMS

- `shipaurora/cms` is the content management system this site runs on: a Filament admin panel at /admin, pages built from blocks in a visual editor, a media library, forms, menus, translations and AI tools (an in-panel assistant and an MCP server).
- The website is the app's own code: its page layouts (Blade views in `resources/views/vendor/aurora/layouts`, published on install, and layouts of its own in `config('aurora.layout_path')`, `app/Layouts` by default, made with `php artisan make:aurora-layout`), its blocks (in `config('aurora.block_path')`, `app/Blocks` by default, made with `php artisan aurora:make-block`, with views in `resources/views/components/blocks`) and its settings. Aurora's code lives in the package; never edit it in `vendor/`, use the hooks it offers.
- Always activate the `aurora-development` skill when creating or changing blocks, page layouts and their fields (`Layouts::schema()`), the website's settings tabs, the page view, or anything that uses `Aurora\` classes.
- Activate the `aurora-porting` skill when moving an existing website (static HTML, WordPress or another CMS, a Laravel app) onto Aurora: it decides what becomes a layout, a block, a setting or a model of the site's own, and how the content comes across.
- In a project that runs Statamic (`statamic/cms` installed), "port this to Aurora" means the `aurora-statamic` skill: it ports the site in place, beside Statamic, and switches it over at the end.
- Porting a Netflex site (`netflex/framework` or `netflex/sdk` in the old project) means the `aurora-netflex` skill: it rebuilds the site in a new Aurora app and brings the content across from the Netflex API.
- Aurora has no shop, customer register or newsletters. When a site needs one (or the site being ported has one), ask the user what it becomes before building it, and offer what section 4 of the `aurora-porting` skill recommends: Shopify for a shop, headless, so the shop is served on the website and only the checkout is Shopify's (or a shop engine in Aurora's admin: Vanilo, or Lunar once it installs); a `Customer` model of the site's own; campaigns made in the admin and sent by Brevo or Mailchimp.
- Every block gets a screenshot made from its example data, for the visual editor's block list. To generate the missing ones, follow "Give every block a screenshot" in the `aurora-development` skill (`php artisan aurora:block-previews --missing --json`).
- Blocks paint the brand with Tailwind's `brand-*` and `accent-*` colours (defined in the site's `@theme`), never fixed colours, so they follow the brand colours editors set under Settings › Website › Branding: see "Brand colours" in the `aurora-development` skill.
